// User XKB rules — read by libxkbcommon (so: sway), not by X11's xkbcomp.
//
// libxkbcommon searches $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xkb BEFORE /usr/share/X11/xkb, so this
// file shadows the system `evdev` ruleset for every keymap sway compiles. It
// must therefore stay a thin wrapper: include the system ruleset unchanged,
// then declare the custom options. Anything left out here is simply gone.
//
// ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the opposite of the arrangement
// libxkbcommon's user-configuration example shows for custom *layouts*. The
// symbols each matching rule contributes are concatenated in rule order, and
// the last writer of a key wins. With the custom block first, the system's
// `inet(evdev)` — which the include drags in for every model, and which is
// what puts XF86Tools on <FK13> — lands afterwards and silently overwrites the
// custom key definition. Measured: the modifier_map survived, the keysym did
// not. Include first, custom last, and the override holds.
//
// An option declared here does nothing until some device asks for it by name,
// so adding one is safe for every other keyboard.

! include %S/evdev

! option = symbols
  custom:f13_super = +custom(f13_super)
  custom:swap_esc_compose = +custom(swap_esc_compose)
